{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"write-lab-meeting-update","kind":"prompt","title":"Write a lab meeting update","description":"Turns a week or month of bench notes into a short lab meeting update with the question, what was done, results with honest caveats, what failed, decisions needed and next steps.","category":"lab-work","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review"],"role":["researcher","student"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["outline","summary"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"quick","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"optional","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["lab-meeting","progress-update","research-update","slides-outline","supervisor-meeting"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-lab-notebook-entry","troubleshoot-failed-experiment","plan-research-talk"]},"args":[{"name":"notes","description":"Your notes since the last update - experiments run, results (numbers or descriptions of figures), what failed, and what you are unsure about.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"format","description":"How long and in what form, for example \"5 slides, 10 minutes\" or \"a written paragraph for the PI's weekly email\".","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"ask","description":"Decisions or help you need from the group or PI. Optional.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Headline","Slide or section plan","Results and caveats","What did not work","Decisions needed","Next steps"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nLab meeting updates go best when they start from the question, show the key data with the controls visible, are honest about n and replication, and end with a specific ask. Students often present everything they did in chronological order, hide failed experiments, or claim a result from a single run. Failed experiments shown with a hypothesis about why are often the most useful part, because the group can help. A one-line headline at the start helps a busy PI.\n</context>\n\n<task>\n<notes>\n{{notes}}\n</notes>\n\nFormat: {{format}}\n{{#ask}}\n\n<ask>\n{{ask}}\n</ask>\n{{/ask}}\n\n1. Headline: one sentence on where the project stands.\n2. Plan the slides or sections to fit the format: the question and why it matters (one slide at most for a regular update), what was done, key results, what did not work, decisions needed, next steps.\n3. Results and caveats: for each result, the figure or table to show, controls on the same figure, n and whether it is biological or technical replication, and a caveat if the result is from a single run.\n4. What did not work: the failure, what the controls suggest, and what you plan to try.\n5. Decisions needed: turn the ask (or what the notes imply) into specific questions for the group.\n6. Next steps with rough timing.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not strengthen results beyond the notes: no \"significant\" without a test, no \"confirmed\" from one run.\n- Do not invent numbers or figures; refer to \"your figure of X\" where the notes describe one.\n- Fit the length to the format given.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Headline\n## Slide or section plan\nNumbered, with a title and one-line content for each.\n## Results and caveats\n## What did not work\n## Decisions needed\n## Next steps\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
